  "readingTopicFertility": "Fertility",
  "linksCycle": "Periods|NHS|https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/periods/\nPeriod pain|NHS|https://www.nhs.uk/symptoms/period-pain/\nPMS (premenstrual syndrome)|NHS|https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/pre-menstrual-syndrome/\nEndometriosis|NHS|https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/endometriosis/\nThrush|NHS|https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/thrush-in-men-and-women/",
  "linksSexuality": "Orgasms and pleasure|Brook|https://www.brook.org.uk/your-life/orgasms/\nMasturbation|Brook|https://www.brook.org.uk/your-life/masturbation/\nAnal sex|Brook|https://www.brook.org.uk/your-life/anal-sex/\nPainful sex|Brook|https://www.brook.org.uk/your-life/painful-sex/\nLow sex drive|NHS|https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/loss-of-libido/\nVaginal dryness|NHS|https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vaginal-dryness/\nSexually transmitted infections|NHS|https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/sexually-transmitted-infections-stis/\nSexual health|World Health Organization|https://www.who.int/health-topics/sexual-health",
  "linksContraception": "Contraception|NHS|https://www.nhs.uk/contraception/\nEmergency contraception|NHS|https://www.nhs.uk/contraception/emergency-contraception/",
  "linksFertility": "Trying for a baby|NHS|https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/trying-for-a-baby/\nInfertility|NHS|https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/infertility/",
  "linksPerimenopause": "Menopause and perimenopause|NHS|https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/menopause/",
  "linksPregnancy": "Pregnancy|NHS|https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/\nSex and contraception after birth|NHS|https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/baby/support-and-services/sex-and-contraception-after-birth/",
  "guideIntOrgasmTitle": "Orgasm: all your questions",
  "guideIntOrgasmBody": "Fireworks for some, a quiet wave for others, a no-show for many: orgasm has a thousand faces, and none is the right one.\n\nFor most women, it all starts with the clitoris. What you see is only the tip: it runs on inside, on either side of the vagina. That is why penetration alone is rarely enough, and why a hand, a tongue or a toy on top changes everything.\n\nVaginal or clitoral? An old debate: it is the same organ, reached by different roads. Together, multiple, loud? Nice extras, not goals.\n\nWhat really helps: time, being turned on before you start, a head that is not elsewhere, and someone, you included, who knows where and how.",
  "guideIntNoOrgasmTitle": "I can't come: so what?",
  "guideIntNoOrgasmBody": "You fake it, or you wait for it while wondering what is wrong. Spoiler: probably nothing.\n\nMany women rarely or never come from penetration alone. It is not a manufacturing fault: it is anatomy.\n\nThe usual brakes are not in the body: the pressure to get there, tiredness, the shopping list scrolling by, a partner in a hurry, the habit of coming second.\n\nWhere to start: alone, with nothing at stake, to find what does something for you. Then show it or say it. And take orgasm off the list of things to achieve: it comes more readily when nobody is waiting at the door.",
  "guideIntSoloTitle": "Touching yourself: the manual nobody gave you",
  "guideIntSoloBody": "Hardly anyone talks about it, nearly everyone does it. Masturbating is neither a consolation prize nor cheating: it is the surest way to know what you like.\n\nThere is no right method. Fingers, the palm, the shower head, a pillow, a toy: the clitoris likes steadiness more than force, and often the edges more than the centre.\n\nChange the pressure, the rhythm, the position. Add lube: it changes everything. Take your time, or not: five minutes before sleep count too.\n\nDuring your period? Yes, and some say it eases their cramps. In a relationship? Yes too: one takes nothing from the other.",
  "guideIntPeriodSexTitle": "Sex on your period: yes, and here is how",
  "guideIntPeriodSexBody": "Nothing forbids it, many want it, and desire sometimes rises right then. What is left is logistics.\n\nA dark towel on the bed, a shower for two, a position where you lie down: the flow fades into the background. Tampons and cups come out before penetration.\n\nBlood helps things glide, orgasm relaxes: some find their period easier to live with afterwards. Others want nothing at all, and that is just as valid.\n\nSay it plainly: \"I'm on my period, is that OK with you?\" Most partners care far less than you think.",
  "guideIntAnalTitle": "Anal sex: wanting it, getting ready, staying comfortable",
  "guideIntAnalBody": "Curious, tempted, or asked without being sure you want to? First rule: it only happens if you want it.\n\nThe anus makes no lubrication and does not relax on command. So: a lot of lube, a lot of time, and start small. A finger, a small toy with a flared base, long before anything else.\n\nBreathe, and push out slightly at the moment of entry: the muscle opens. You set the pace. A position where you control the depth helps a lot.\n\nIt should not hurt. Some discomfort at first, yes. Pain: stop, add lube, try again later, or never.\n\nAs for cleanliness, a shower is enough. The rest is part of the game, and everybody knows it.",
  "guideIntLubeTitle": "Lube: the best friend nobody dares to buy",
  "guideIntLubeBody": "It is not an admission of failure. It is what turns \"fine\" into \"more\".\n\nWater-based: goes with everything, dries faster, just add more. Silicone-based: glides for a long time, perfect in the shower and for anal, but not with silicone toys. Oils and petroleum jelly: lovely for a massage, to be kept away from latex condoms.\n\nWhen? As soon as it drags, for touching as for penetration, and always for anal. How wet you get changes with your cycle, stress, tiredness, breastfeeding, age: it says nothing about how much you want it.\n\nA tip: on you and on the other person, and the bottle within reach rather than at the back of the drawer.",
  "guideIntDesireCycleTitle": "Your desire has its seasons",
  "guideIntDesireCycleBody": "Some days you would climb the curtains, others you would rather have your series. Your cycle often has something to do with it.\n\nMany women feel their desire rise around the middle of the cycle and drop before their period. Others peak just before, or during. No calendar fits everyone.\n\nYours can be read in your journal: log your desire for a few cycles, and the app shows your high days. Handy to plan a weekend for two, or to understand a \"not tonight\" without making a thing of it.\n\nAnd then there is everything that is not hormonal: tiredness, mental load, an argument left open. The cycle tells part of the story, not all of it.",
  "guideIntSquirtingTitle": "Squirting: what is known, what is fantasy",
  "guideIntSquirtingBody": "A star on screen, a mystery in bedrooms: female ejaculation intrigues, and many wonder whether they \"should\" manage it.\n\nSome women release fluid at a moment of intense pleasure, sometimes a lot, sometimes a few drops. Others never do. Researchers do not all agree on what it contains or where exactly it comes from.\n\nWhat is certain: it is neither proof of orgasm, nor a higher level, nor a performance to offer. And what you see in porn is staged.\n\nIf it happens to you: a towel, and no shame. If it does not: you are missing nothing.",
  "guideIntPainTitle": "When it hurts",
  "guideIntPainBody": "Gritting your teeth until it is over was never part of the deal. Sex that hurts gets sorted, not endured.\n\nMost often it is a matter of time and glide: not turned on enough, not enough lube, penetration too soon or too deep. Slowing down, changing the angle, adding lube is often enough.\n\nSometimes the body closes by itself, even when the head says yes. Sometimes the pain is deep, or comes back at certain times of the cycle. Then it is no longer about technique.\n\nSay stop as soon as it hurts. Someone who cares about you would a thousand times rather stop.",
  "guideIntTearsTitle": "Crying after sex",
  "guideIntTearsBody": "It was good, and yet the tears come. Or a low mood drops out of nowhere.\n\nIt happens to a lot of people. After a strong build-up, the body comes down: tension lets go, and sometimes it overflows.\n\nIt does not mean you are sad, or that something is wrong between the two of you. Sometimes it does: the body knows before the head that you did not really want to.\n\nIn the moment, say it plainly: \"I don't know why, it will pass\". Ask for arms or for air, whichever does you good.",
  "guideIntSayItTitle": "Saying what you like, and what you don't",
  "guideIntSayItBody": "Nobody reads minds, not even in bed. And \"a bit to the left\" never killed the mood.\n\nThe simplest: guide as you go. A hand on theirs, a \"yes, like that\", a \"softer\". Short words work better than long speeches.\n\nFor bigger subjects, a new wish or something you no longer like, pick a moment with clothes on: in the car, on a walk, not right after.\n\nStart with what you like: \"I love it when you…\". Then the wish: \"I would like to try…\". And a no stays a no, both ways.",
  "guideIntNoDesireTitle": "No desire at all: where to start again",
  "guideIntNoDesireBody": "You love them, you find them attractive, and yet: nothing. Low desire is one of the most shared subjects, and the least admitted.\n\nLook at life first: broken sleep, mental load, a grudge that lingers, a body you do not like these days. Desire needs room, and there is none.\n\nFor many women, wanting does not fall from the sky: it comes along the way, once the touching has started. Waiting to want it before you begin can mean waiting a long time.\n\nWhat restarts it: time together with no goal, touch that leads to nothing required, something new, and moments alone with your own body.",
  "guideIntAfterBabyTitle": "After a baby: sex comes back, or not just yet",
  "guideIntAfterBabyBody": "Between broken nights, a body that has changed and a baby attached to you, desire has good reasons to keep you waiting.\n\nThere is no date. Some start again after a few weeks, others after several months. The right time is when you want to and it does not hurt.\n\nThe body often makes less lubrication, especially while breastfeeding: lube becomes essential. Start with touching, and keep control of the pace.\n\nAnd the two of you? Say where you are, even if it is \"not now\". Tenderness with nothing required after it bridges the gap.",
  "guideIntMidlifeTitle": "Perimenopause: sex changes its rules",
  "guideIntMidlifeBody": "No desire, or desire but it chafes? In perimenopause, desire plays hide and seek and the body makes less lubrication. It says nothing about your relationship, or about you.\n\nWhat changes: it often takes more time, more touching, and lube without a second thought. What does not: your ability to feel pleasure.\n\nMany women describe freer sex at this age: you know what you like, you dare to say it, you no longer pretend.\n\nBroken nights and hot flushes do not help desire. Looking after them is also looking after your sex life.",
  "guideIntOnScheduleTitle": "Trying for a baby: when sex becomes a schedule",
  "guideIntOnScheduleBody": "At first it was exciting. Three months later it is \"tonight, we have to\". Desire on demand: nobody likes that.\n\nIt is very common, and it says nothing about your relationship. Pressure cuts desire, for you as for the other.\n\nWhat lightens it: not staking everything on one evening, keeping moments with nothing at stake, and now and then putting the calendar away.\n\nTell the other one: \"I want you, not only a baby\". It changes the mood.",
